Account for construction queries and returned data together (@computing-index-cost: 0, is #chapter, #hephaestus-core "Investigate-a-stable-catalog-with-independent-search-evidence.lingua#r_0E2277D89596295190BB7B99BC") { r_029F87EE3AF507AEAD87F5CC8D
Reuse [Count work and storage under an explicit cost model](Count-work-and-storage-under-an-explicit-cost-model.lingua#r_056CD81F8DB8FE38538185D3DC): an operation count is a claim under an explicit model. Start with unit-cost key comparisons, constant-cost indexed access, and references of bounded size. Count construction comparisons, shifted references, query comparisons and returned references separately. Validation and conversion traverse all n rows even if construction makes no shifts. The example does not instrument allocation, loop-control instructions, character comparisons, cache behavior or elapsed time.

Insertion into a prefix of length i shifts at most i items and makes at most i key comparisons. Summing i from 1 to n−1 gives n(n−1)/2, a quadratic worst-case bound for construction. Strictly descending distinct keys attain both counts. Already ordered keys use n−1 comparisons and zero shifts for n≥1, while validation and conversion still take linear work. The stored catalog occupies space proportional to n row references plus its normalized row storage; the caller's original collection may coexist with these allocations.

For either boundary search, a positive width w becomes at most floor(w/2) after one comparison. After t comparisons it is at most floor(n/2^t). The largest number of comparisons is bounded by the smallest integer t with 2^t>n, with zero for n=0. For example, n=7 needs at most three comparisons per boundary and n=8 at most four. This count is also ceil(log2(n+1)); the notation describes the number of repeated halvings, rather than introducing a new algorithm. Two boundaries therefore use at most twice that count.

Producing a k-row result still requires up to linear copying work in k and space for k references. Thus lookup has an upper bound proportional to log(n+1)+k under this model, with fixed call overhead even when n=0. The shorthand O(g(n)) says that an upper bound grows no faster than a constant multiple of g beyond a fixed threshold; it does not give elapsed seconds or say every input attains that bound. A full scan costs n equality comparisons plus output construction.

For q queries, compare one construction plus all searches and outputs with validation plus q scans and their outputs. Worst-case insertion construction gives a total upper bound of O(n² + q log(n+1) + sum of output sizes), with fixed and validation terms understood; scanning gives O(n + qn + sum of output sizes). Better sorting and other representations can change the construction term. A mapping to groups can honor duplicate-preserving lookup, but its construction, equality/hash assumptions, storage and ordered-range behavior need their own analysis. Later lessons develop those alternatives.

Our four-row fixture needs three construction comparisons and one shift. Its b query needs five binary-search comparisons but only four scan comparisons. This counterexample prevents the false claim that binary search always does less work on every input. A long equal-key group can also make output cost dominate either method. If the maximum key length is L, string comparison can inspect up to order L characters; a bound counting comparisons alone hides that dependence. Arbitrary-precision integer payload storage depends on magnitude even though ordering ignores payloads. Python list access is not the access cost of a linked structure, a disk file or a remote service.

**Work:** derive the triangular sum by pairing first and last terms, compute the halving bound for sizes 0–9, and count q=1 and q=100 queries under declared inputs. Propose a timing experiment that reports interpreter, hardware, input families and repeated measurements without treating timings as a universal complexity proof. Read MIT lecture 2's representation costs and lecture 3's insertion analysis through [Introduction to Algorithms — MIT 6.006, Spring 2020](../sources/Introduction-to-Algorithms-MIT-6006.lingua#r_04AEC4B446A81994AEEB84801D); full amortization and recurrence theory remain later formation.

